PALSWAP  VERSION 1.4   NOTICE ARTWORK UTILITY
CONTINUITY GRAPHICS SUPPORT, TERMINAL SEVEN
==============================================================

4.  THE EDIT ORDER TABLE

    PALSWAP does not store the sixteen palette entries in
    numeric order. Entries are stored in EDIT ORDER: the entry
    most recently written by an editing station is filed first.

    Recovering the table for a notice therefore requires BOTH
    revisions of that notice, the current copy and the retained
    prior copy, and not either one on its own.

5.  READING A NOTICE AGAINST THE TABLE

    A notice is read against its own table. The result is the
    DESIGNATION for that notice, sixteen characters.

6.  SEAL CHECK

    A recovered designation is correct only if its CRC-32,
    computed over the sixteen characters as stored, equals the
    seal check for the notice. For notice 10 that value is:

        SEAL CHK 34887E5B

7.  THE CLEARANCE BLOCK

    The clearance for this notice is held sealed below. The block
    is sealed against the DESIGNATION by byte-wise exclusive-or,
    the designation being repeated as many times as required to
    cover the block. Unsealing therefore requires the designation
    exactly as recovered, in upper case, sixteen characters.

        35 25 2D 22 35 3A 64 31 63 2E 7A 11
        35 7F 5D 67 61 78 78 23 78 65 64 27
        61 29 34

    An unsealed clearance is correct only if its CRC-32 equals:

        BLOCK CHK E2021920

==============================================================
END PALSWAP DOCUMENTATION
